<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="c7c444be">&lt;p&gt;
    Attributes contain information about requirements to help obtain clarity. When captured properly, they can provide
    insight on the overall state of a system. The right level of attributes to capture will be determined in conjunction
    with the client. The overall goal is to get to the right level of detail so there is sufficient information without
    unnecessary detail.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Attributes can be pre-defined up front of they can be free form text (e.g., priority would be pre-defined to high,
    medium or low and requirement description would be free form text).&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="d57602bf"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="18b3e77a">&lt;p&gt;
    Capturing requirement attributes can be an iterative process.  Revisit each requirement regularly throughout the
    development lifecycle to update or populate missing attributes.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="8817366e"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="46d5acff">&lt;p&gt;
    To detail the information characteristics needed to define and act upon requirements.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="impactOfNotHaving" nodeId="c4fe1a59">Attribute definition allows each requirement, as an object, to be distinguished from other requirements.  In addition
to its textual description or modeling notation, each requirement should have supporting pieces of information, or
attributes associated with it.</attribute>
<attribute name="reasonsForNotNeeding" nodeId="3fa05ac4"></attribute>
<attribute name="briefOutline" nodeId="cf9adb47">&lt;p&gt;
    The minimal attributes to capture include:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Date the requirement was created
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Current version number (or other ID format) of the requirement
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Author who wrote the requirement
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Priority
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Status
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Origin or source of the requirement
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Rationale behind the requirement
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Release number or iteration to which the requirement is allocated
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Stakeholder to contact with questions or to make decisions about proposed changes
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Validation method to be used or acceptance criteria&lt;br /&gt;
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="representationOptions" nodeId="a427ba1e">Requirements Management tooling typically provides several system-generated attributes in addition to allowing
configuration of others.  These tools allow database querying and reporting options to customize attribute
presentation.</attribute>
<attribute name="representation" nodeId="c71a3678"></attribute>
